Z||000001||alpha||12-17-2000||05:12 PM||||If it is not a rust bucket, that would be a cool car. I love Z-Cars. It probably has an automatic transmission in because of the swap though.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://fiero.dhs.org/fiero/sig.jpg">   <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu">douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||12-19-2000||02:17 PM||||My dad has a '75 280Z with a L99 V8 (4.3 litre LT1 style) from a Caprice. He uses a Saginaw (sp?) 4 spd with Borg-Warner planetary overdrive. There isn't a rubber bushing still in the thing and it has front and rear swaybars. It also has 14x7 Supra wheels that have been modified to fit. The car uses Macpherson strut suspension front and Chapman strut rear.<BR>     It has about the same level of limit cornering grip as a Fiero, but a well maintined Fiero is definitely better through slalom and rapid directional transition manuevers. With that V8 the car obviously accelerates much better than a stock Fiero.<BR>     It is a blast to drive, and a definite sleeper since it has ratty paint.<BR>     I'd say go for it! There is a book called "How to V8 your Datsun" that would tell you a lot of what to look for on the car. The cars are simple and easy to work on, and have a fair number of performance upgrades available (like four wheel disk brakes); even a couple of Ferrari body kits.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
